00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.530
You know

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.570
if you were

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:04.450
given

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.577
if Mike Trout came into your
office and said you know,

00:01:08.577 --> 00:01:11.350
"I want you to make me

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.120
a household name.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.057
I want you to make me Twitter famous."

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.270
What would you tell him?

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.700
- I would start with a series of questions

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.480
for about 15 to 20 minutes

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.590
because you have to
reverse engineer someone

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.200
who's actually busy and famous.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.230
You have to reverse engineer their DNA.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.770
So for example, Mike
could have those ambitions

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.290
yet may feel uncomfortable
in being out there

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.030
because he may think it's bragging

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.420
'cause that's just his DNA.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.520
And I would explain fine

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.070
maybe the format that we need
to put you in is a podcast

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.710
where you feel more comfortable

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.920
'cause your shooting the shit with friends

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.580
and that's how were going to
be able to create content.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.660
Some people are super
comfortable running around Earth

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.130
taking selfies

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.170
and that's where they create content.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.670
Other people, look- look
at myself, I'm busy.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.690
I'm running an actual company that's big.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:09.940
I don't

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.800
Even though I have a huge audience

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.430
and have made myself "big"

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.940
by having somebody film me at all times

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.960
to create content to be distributed

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.870
so the first thing that has to happen

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.600
is there's a very big DNA trait difference

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.890
in all these people.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.740
I would try to figure
out how to create content

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.190
in a world where he was
comfortable in doing that

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.550
because most people feel
like they don't have the time

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.550
or ironically even though
there in the public eye

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.220
don't like it for many different reasons:

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.180
skeletons in the closet,
not their DNA trait,

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.510
they were taught to be
humble by their grandpa

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.500
like there's a million reasons why.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.250
But I would ask him a series of questions

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:52.990
that put me in a situation

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.720
that allowed me to create content out of

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.490
and preferably in video form

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:03.490
which would then feed
written and audio form

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.190
in the way that I think about it.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.310
- [Jack] If you could, you
know, sort of walk me through

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.133
what personality traits
align with what format.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.360
- I think the more extroverted you are

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.070
the more video,

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:17.950
vlogging,

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.020
or having actually somebody
follow you around with a camera

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.080
which takes a lot of

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.730
a lot of internal understanding
to be okay with that

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:32.330
because it could come across
you know like being a charlatan

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.380
or you know

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.080
its a real personality trait

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.540
which I have a lot of empathy.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.300
You know when I started
doing that three years ago

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.690
I knew that it was gonna
be like frowned upon

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:44.600
but now it's happening
at scale on YouTube.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.000
In it's most basic form,
extrovert: video and picture

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.600
introvert: it's being
recorded behind the scenes

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.750
manifesting as audio and written.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.450
- [Jack] How much do you work
with particular individuals

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.620
and how much do you work with brand?

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.100
- So we work more with brands
at Vayner Media and I do

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.210
but with individuals we have a
division called Vayner Talent

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.623
where we have 12, 13 clients
that we do this with.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.530
And then as a human being, I'm
spending quite a bit of time

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.320
with awfully famous
individuals off the record

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.300
in giving them my points of view

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.410
on how they could do better
on socials and how they say it

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.340
but its far more holistic than that.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.203
It's being a media entity.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.740
- [Jack] When you're working with brands

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.450
is there a type of brand

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.450
that you see perhaps
aligning with Mike Trout,

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.310
you know and his characteristics
as an athlete and a person?

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.240
- You know whether it's performance based

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.760
'cause he's a super athlete

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.020
or back to the point of it

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.550
he's so not out there or super extroverted

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.250
I'm sure law firms and banks
and insurance companies

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.240
and you know

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.180
professional service companies would love

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:07.180
to have a clean-faced, clean
record, super athlete, you know

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.030
nice boy be the face of their brand.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:12.930
- [Jack] Especially
the sport like baseball

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.670
where you know there's not as
much focus on the individual

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:20.430
as there is in the NBA
where you have someone

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.920
whose personality is not obviously outside

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.990
it's a heavier lift.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.180
Has that been true in your experience?

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.350
- I think that is way

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:33.310
I think you just walked

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:36.280
into the much, much, much bigger problem

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.825
at hand for baseball.

00:05:38.825 --> 00:05:39.658
- [Jack] Uh huh.

00:05:39.658 --> 00:05:43.420
- I don't believe that that
is a product of anything

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.360
other than the way Major League Baseball

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.940
has handled themselves versus the NBA.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.600
I mean Reggie Jackson
had a candy bar in 1977.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.510
Let's not get confused
what's happening here.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.090
What's happening here is
that Major League Baseball

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.940
a decade ago decided to
make short-term economics

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:03.840
by creating BAM, their media group,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.830
so that they can control
all the short-term dollars

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.760
at the detriment of being
everywhere where people are

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.950
and building up their individuals.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:17.640
They've suffocated the creativity
in a digital environment

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.010
over the last decade.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.900
There's plenty of
personalities in baseball.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.080
The way that the team's
have to do their creative

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.780
in social media

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.690
and the way that you can't
find any baseball content

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:33.270
on the internet unless
Major League Baseball

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:36.510
is making a piece of that
action through their accounts

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.730
has created a culture of suppression

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:43.440
of personal brands.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.450
I mean literally to the point of

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.305
a fan of

00:06:47.305 --> 00:06:49.470
Kolten Wong

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:50.880
creating content on Instagram

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.033
potentially is gonna be taken
down by Major League Baseball.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.290
Steph Curry was built not only
by Steph Curry and by the NBA

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.810
Steph Curry was built by
the fans creating content

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.110
of Steph Curry in the trenches
where attention actually is.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.500
There was plenty of interest
in Derek Jeter and A-Rod

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:08.513
in real life.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.380
Bryce Harper is loaded with everything

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:13.600
that you'd be interested in.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.720
It's suppressed out of him systematically.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.420
- [Jack] So if you were
commissioner for day,

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:22.660
what would you change
about the League's approach

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:23.830
to digital?

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:28.830
- I would open up the communistic
point of view dramatically

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.250
and have far more partnerships

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.890
and leeway with the actual audience

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:39.740
and I would disproportionately
be petrified

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:43.300
that six to 13 year olds in America

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:47.480
consider Messi, Ronaldo, LeBron, Curry,

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.690
and Ninja the Fortnite
player way more famous

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.980
than any single baseball player on Earth.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.370
That is going to play itself out

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:59.050
in an extremely negative
way in a 40 year window

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:01.020
and like every other business on Earth

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:06.020
baseball decided to treat
digital for short-term money

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:09.193
instead long-term IP
and success and brand.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:11.160
- [Jack] I mean the thing this like

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.420
I think about baseball fandom
as oh it gets passed down

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.340
you know from father to son

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.430
and you root for you
know a particular team

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:24.820
and if that skipped a generation

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.930
or if that is skipping a generation

00:08:28.074 --> 00:08:31.830
you know is that salvageable?

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.010
- It's not.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:34.220
There gonna take the short-term

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:35.080
There gonna take the short

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.690
It's funny how I think about short-term.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:40.020
I was literally about to say
they take the short-term loss

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.010
which is actually a 30 year window.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.113
It's salvageable if they
start now with you know

00:08:46.113 --> 00:08:47.620
with fathers and sons,

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:49.820
mothers and daughters
interested in baseball

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.680
having the ability to pass it on

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.900
in a YouTube, Snapchat, app

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.400
you know how they

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.660
Baseball said "You're
gonna consume us the way

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.650
we baseball want, not the way you want."

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:05.130
And the problem for them is it was great

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.040
for the short-term, you know,

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.970
and it looked great like
they're making all this money

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:13.476
but what they did was
fundamentally destroyed

00:09:13.476 --> 00:09:16.770
the 50 year window of
their sport in a real way.

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:20.270
People are unbelievably naive
how this is gonna play out.

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.840
It's an incredible sport to
take the family out for the day.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:24.880
That's nice.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.910
So is soccer during the same time of year.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:28.810
Like this is not going to work out

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.270
for baseball in a 50 year window

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.277
and it starts with do you care.

00:09:32.277 --> 00:09:36.220
And I think that the answer
is no I do not believe so.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.820
I couldn't be more down on
baseball 35 years from now

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.470
based on what they're doing now.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.170
Kids are not growing up with
baseball cards like they did.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.000
Their attention's in different places.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.030
There's new alternatives
like everyone's like

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:51.740
well it's a great summer
night, well guess what

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.840
do you know what every eight
to 13 year old in America

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:55.150
is doing on summer nights right now

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.770
they're staying inside
and playing Fortnite.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:58.603
- [Jack] Yeah.

00:09:58.603 --> 00:10:00.230
- So I don't care if you're
good for the outside.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:02.130
If nobody's going outside you're finished.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.930
And guess what I think
people need a history lesson.

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:07.710
Boxing and horse racing
were the most popular sports

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.720
along with baseball in this
country in 1920, 30, and 40

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.560
and they were radio sports, that's why.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.160
And then television came along

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.510
and football and basketball
were television sports.

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.150
And guess what they've benefited.

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.200
And guess what, now
we're in a digital world

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.840
and that's who's going
to benefit a.k.a E-sports

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:26.420
a.k.a. things like the NBA

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.100
that understand how to manipulate it.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:29.170
And who's going to lose?

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:30.003
Baseball.

00:10:30.003 --> 00:10:30.836
You know why,

00:10:30.836 --> 00:10:32.310
'cause they make an extra
hundred million dollars

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:35.640
in little pennies and nickels
in ads on their channels

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.870
that they rev share with
YouTube or Facebook.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.870
Yet nobodies going there
and it's not cultural

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:43.100
and nobody gives a crap.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.870
And then classic NHL
that's always been bad

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:49.330
decides to sign up with BAM.

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:51.410
I mean talk about a sport

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.750
that barely holding on to relevance.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:55.200
And so what they did--

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.137
What they did was literally
go into the same drug

00:10:59.137 --> 00:11:00.950
and sign up with BAM media

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.900
which is devastating to
me because I adore hockey.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:04.680
Super hard to get excited about something

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.910
if you can't see it.

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.180
And I don't wanna hear like

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:08.710
well players have accounts on social.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:12.520
They sure do, but when the
team's content is sucked

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:14.680
into being controlled by the organization

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.700
like it doesn't fester a culture.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:20.410
The Padres don't have a
culture of creating content

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.110
that's really progressive

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:24.590
because it's part of the BAM machine

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:27.800
so they're sucking off the
teat of BAM and there's

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.000
and so then all of a sudden

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.430
if there's not a culture
inside the organization

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:35.130
well the players don't feel a
natural kinship towards that.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.340
And it's crazy because
baseball is super cultural.

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:39.660
Matt Kemp dated Rihanna.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.480
Like they have nothing
but ammo culturally.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:43.350
You know what I mean.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.640
- [Jack] No, and I think
they've certainly succeeded in

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.580
I mean maybe there's some pushback

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.750
but I do think baseball has succeeded

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:55.710
in making it seem like
the player's problem

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.020
as opposed to the league's problem.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:00.713
- So to me that's a really great,

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:05.900
great conversation because anytime

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:06.770
a leader

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:11.880
says their employees are at fault

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:15.110
there in the beginning of
the process of the end.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.315
- [Jack] Yeah.

00:12:16.315 --> 00:12:20.900
- The utter lack of
accountability is laughable.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.720
Baseball took the money in
the short-term in digital

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:27.230
and they destroyed the long-term
value of the entire thing

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:28.170
because of it.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:29.323
The end.

